{
  "id": "COMPOUND:14268 - PROTEIN:4723",
  "verbalization": "Benzocaine is implicated in the regulation of Serine/threonine-protein kinase Nek4 through a series of molecular interactions involving K-252a. The evidence suggests that benzocaine acts as a target of K-252a, which in turn modulates Nek4. This pathway is supported by multiple independent connections, with benzocaine directly targeting different proteins—such as Glutamine--fructose-6-phosphate aminotransferase and Solute carrier organic anion transporter family member 2A1—each of which is also a target of K-252a. The consistent presence of K-252a as a mediator between benzocaine and Nek4 underscores a potential role for this kinase in the cellular response to benzocaine. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible mechanism by which benzocaine influences Nek4, likely through the activation or inhibition of K-252a. The evidence supports a central role for K-252a in linking benzocaine to Nek4, with the exact nature of the interaction remaining to be fully elucidated.",
